d two lines that each pass through a point in column 2 and a point in column 4. Let's see possibilities:\n\n- Pair (4,1) with (2,b): slopes:\n  - (2,1): slope = (1-1)/(4-2) = 0, not sunny.\n  - (2,2): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny.\n  - (2,3): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny.\n  - (2,4): slope = (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2, sunny.\n\nSo (4,1) can be paired with (2,2) (slope -1/2) or (2,4) (slope -3/2). But (2,2) already used for (5,1) maybe. So we could use (2,4) for (4,1).\n\n- Pair (4,2) with (2,b):\n  - (2,1): slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2, sunny.\n  - (2,2): slope = (2-2)/(4-2) = 0, not sunny.\n  - (2,3): slope = (2-3)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny.\n  - (2,4): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny.\n\nSo (4,2) can be paired with (2,1) (slope 1/2) or (2,3) (slope -1/2). So we can assign (4,2) to line with (2,1) or (2,3). Good.\n\nNow column 3 has points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We need to cover these with the remaining lines. We have 4 lines total. So far we have assigned:\n\n- L1: (2,2) with (5,1) (slope -1/3)\n- L2: (2,4) with (4,1) (slope -3/2)\n- L3: (2,1) with (4,2) (slope 1/2)\n- L4: (2,3) maybe with (3,?) or something.\n\nWe need to cover column 3 points. Let's see which points are left uncovered: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). Also we need to ensure each line covers at most one point per column. Let's check each line's column assignments:\n\n- L1: (2,2) (col2) and (5,1) (col5). So L1 covers col2 and col5.\n- L2: (2,4) (col2) and (4,1) (col4). So L2 covers col2 and col4.\n- L3: (2,1) (col2) and (4,2) (col4). So L3 covers col2 and col4.\n- L4: (2,3) (col2) currently not paired with any other column. So L4 can be used to cover a point in column 3 (or column 4, but column 4 already covered by L2 and L3). So L4 can pair (2,3) with a point in column 3.\n\nNow we need to cover (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We have only L4 left to cover them, but each line can cover at most one point per column, so L4 can cover at most one point in column 3. So we cannot cover all three points in column 3 with